{"_comment": "Employment by occupation and median usual weekly earnings, split by NATIVITY, from the CPS via the BLS foreign-born news release. Held because every other dataset here measures the foreign population and none measured the workers already present, which made the displacement argument one-sided. NATIVITY IS NOT VISA STATUS: 'foreign born' pools naturalised citizens, permanent residents and temporary-visa holders, and 'native born' includes anyone born in the US whatever their parentage. It is far broader than the visa categories used elsewhere and the two must not be treated as one population. THE EARNINGS GAP IS NOT LIKE-FOR-LIKE -- the groups differ in age, education, occupation, region and language and none of it is controlled, so a raw gap describes two populations rather than what an employer pays for the same work. Earnings are MEDIAN USUAL WEEKLY for full-time wage and salary workers, not annual and not including the self-employed. THE TWENTY-YEAR SERIES is annual averages from the CPS via the BLS API, foreign born and native born on the same measure, held as a pair so neither is read alone.", "source": "BLS Current Population Survey, foreign-born workers news release", "source_url": "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/forbrn.nr0.htm", "year": 2025, "earnings_table": "Median usual weekly earnings of full-time wage and salary workers for the foreign born and native born by selected characteristics, 2024-2025 annual averages - 2025 A01 Results", "occupation_table": "Employed foreign-born and native-born people 16 years and over by occupation and sex, 2025 annual averages - 2025 A01 Results", "headline": {"section": null, "foreign_born_thousands": 23709.0, "foreign_born_median_weekly": 1059.0, "native_born_thousands": 97761.0, "native_born_median_weekly": 1236.0, "foreign_as_pct_of_native": 85.7}, "cps_series": {"employed_thousands": {"2005": {"foreign_born": 21022.0, "native_born": 120708.0}, "2006": {"foreign_born": 22225.0, "native_born": 122202.0},